Add `spring-boot-starter-web` to use Spring MVC. Spring Boot then starts an embedded Tomcat server by default and configures the `DispatcherServlet`, request mapping, and Jackson-based JSON support. Add the validation starter when request DTOs use Bean Validation constraints such as `@NotBlank` or `@Positive`.

```xml
<dependencies>
  <dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
  </dependency>
  <dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
  </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

Spring Boot's parent or dependency-management BOM supplies compatible versions, so these two dependency entries normally need no version. The application class is the small entry point that starts Spring Boot:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;

@SpringBootApplication
public class ProductApiApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(ProductApiApplication.class, args);
    }
}
```

By default, component scanning begins in the application class's package and its child packages, so the controller and service should normally live below it. A project generated by Spring Initializr already includes this class and the Spring Boot parent or BOM.

Request and response DTOs make the public contract clear. The create request has no ID because the server creates it; the response includes the new ID. Separate DTOs also stop a database-entity change from silently becoming an API change.

```java
import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotBlank;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotNull;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.Positive;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

record CreateProductRequest(
        @NotBlank String name,
        @NotNull @Positive BigDecimal price) {}

record ProductResponse(
        long id,
        String name,
        BigDecimal price) {}
```

`@NotBlank` rejects a missing, empty, or whitespace-only name. `@NotNull` requires a price, and `@Positive` requires it to be greater than zero. These constraints are applied at the controller boundary when validation support is present and the `@RequestBody` parameter is annotated with `@Valid`.

#### Where request data comes from

| Annotation | Reads from | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `@PathVariable` | URL path | `/products/10` gives `id = 10` |
| `@RequestParam` | Query string | `/products?category=books` |
| `@RequestBody` | Request body | JSON used to create a product |
| `@RequestHeader` | HTTP header | `X-Request-Id: abc-123` |

The controller translates HTTP into an application call. It owns paths, request binding, validation entry points, status codes, headers, and response DTOs. It should not contain pricing rules, stock checks, or database queries.

```java
import jakarta.validation.Valid;
import java.net.URI;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/products")
class ProductController {
    private final ProductService productService;

    ProductController(ProductService productService) {
        this.productService = productService;
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    ProductResponse findById(@PathVariable("id") long id) {
        return productService.findById(id);
    }

    @PostMapping
    ResponseEntity<ProductResponse> create(
            @Valid @RequestBody CreateProductRequest request) {
        ProductResponse product = productService.create(request);
        URI location = URI.create("/products/" + product.id());
        return ResponseEntity.created(location).body(product);
    }
}
```

`@RestController` includes `@Controller` and `@ResponseBody`, so returned objects are handled as response content. The mapping annotations connect an HTTP method and path to each Java method. `@PathVariable("id")` reads the path value explicitly, and `@RequestBody` reads the request body through a suitable message converter.

The single constructor lets Spring inject `ProductService` without `@Autowired`. The create method delegates the use case and then uses `ResponseEntity.created(...)` to produce `201 Created`, a `Location` header, and a response body.

```java
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
class ProductService {
    private final Map<Long, ProductResponse> products =
            new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final AtomicLong nextId = new AtomicLong();

    ProductResponse create(CreateProductRequest request) {
        long id = nextId.incrementAndGet();
        ProductResponse product = new ProductResponse(
                id, request.name(), request.price());
        products.put(id, product);
        return product;
    }

    ProductResponse findById(long id) {
        ProductResponse product = products.get(id);
        if (product == null) {
            throw new ProductNotFoundException(id);
        }
        return product;
    }
}
```

This in-memory map is only a teaching substitute for storage; it is not a repository implementation. A real service can call `ProductRepository` without changing the controller's HTTP contract.

A client needs a meaningful HTTP status and a predictable error body. With validation configured, an invalid request DTO is rejected before the controller method and normally becomes `400 Bad Request`. A missing product becomes `404 Not Found` only when the application maps that condition; throwing an arbitrary exception does not automatically mean 404.

```java
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ExceptionHandler;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestControllerAdvice;

class ProductNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {
    ProductNotFoundException(long id) {
        super("Product " + id + " was not found");
    }
}

record ApiError(String message) {}

@RestControllerAdvice
class ApiExceptionHandler {
    @ExceptionHandler(ProductNotFoundException.class)
    ResponseEntity<ApiError> handleNotFound(
            ProductNotFoundException exception) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
                .body(new ApiError(exception.getMessage()));
    }
}
```

`@RestControllerAdvice` makes this handler available to controllers, and `@ExceptionHandler` selects it for `ProductNotFoundException`. The method turns an application failure into an HTTP response that a client can handle.

#### Status codes used by this API

| Situation | Status | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| Product returned successfully | `200 OK` | The read succeeded |
| Product created | `201 Created` | A new resource was created |
| Request fields are invalid | `400 Bad Request` | The client must correct the input |
| Product ID does not exist | `404 Not Found` | The requested resource is missing |
| Request body has an unsupported media type | `415 Unsupported Media Type` | No converter can read that body as requested |
| Unexpected server failure | `500 Internal Server Error` | The server could not complete the request |

A status code is part of the API contract. Returning `200 OK` for every outcome hides whether the request really succeeded.
